1
00:00:04,543 --> 00:00:07,295
I live in big, beautiful British Columbia,

2
00:00:07,337 --> 00:00:11,841
and my life is fueled by exploring nature
both at home and around the world.

3
00:00:11,883 --> 00:00:14,928
In fact, this is from the Himalaya
just a few months ago

4
00:00:14,970 --> 00:00:16,221
when I was climbing there.

5
00:00:16,263 --> 00:00:17,847
Nature is a huge part of my life

6
00:00:17,889 --> 00:00:20,684
and it inspires my creative
and professional world as well.

7
00:00:20,725 --> 00:00:24,896
I work hard with my team
inspired by nature in Vancouver,

8
00:00:24,896 --> 00:00:26,398
designing buildings.

9
00:00:26,398 --> 00:00:29,693
As an architecture practice,
we have two main missions.

10
00:00:29,693 --> 00:00:33,572
Our first mission is to focus
on making beautiful buildings

11
00:00:33,572 --> 00:00:35,323
that serve our community

12
00:00:35,323 --> 00:00:38,618
and respond to the needs
of the people within them.

13
00:00:38,618 --> 00:00:41,454
The second mission we have
is focused on the fact

14
00:00:41,454 --> 00:00:46,167
that the first mission has a huge impact
on the planet and on climate change,

15
00:00:46,209 --> 00:00:47,836
and we have to work to reduce it.

16
00:00:48,378 --> 00:00:53,300
As a result, our firm is often called
a highly sustainable practice,

17
00:00:53,341 --> 00:00:55,969
and the truth is, it really isn't.

18
00:00:56,011 --> 00:00:58,847
In fact, I don't believe
that word "sustainable building"

19
00:00:58,888 --> 00:01:00,181
or "sustainable practice"

20
00:01:00,223 --> 00:01:02,684
is really true in most cases.

21
00:01:02,726 --> 00:01:04,811
And the reason is the built environment

22
00:01:04,853 --> 00:01:07,188
uses an enormous amount
of the world's resources,

23
00:01:07,188 --> 00:01:10,275
and far too few of those
come from renewable resources.

24
00:01:11,067 --> 00:01:15,155
In fact, the vast majority of the material
that man makes on Earth

25
00:01:15,155 --> 00:01:17,616
goes into the built environment.

26
00:01:17,616 --> 00:01:19,326
The other challenge with buildings

27
00:01:19,326 --> 00:01:22,954
is they represent about 39%
of our greenhouse gas emissions,

28
00:01:22,954 --> 00:01:24,247
or, in North America,

29
00:01:24,289 --> 00:01:26,750
almost half of our
greenhouse gas emissions.

30
00:01:26,791 --> 00:01:30,211
For reference, because
it's not talked about enough,

31
00:01:30,253 --> 00:01:34,966
all of cars, planes, automobiles,
the entire transportation sector combined

32
00:01:35,008 --> 00:01:37,093
represent only about 23%.

33
00:01:37,135 --> 00:01:39,179
So buildings are
a huge part of the problem

34
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:40,555
and not discussed enough.

35
00:01:41,139 --> 00:01:45,018
The reason for that are both the heating
and cooling of buildings, of course,

36
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:47,270
but also the materials
that go into buildings.

37
00:01:47,312 --> 00:01:50,273
And when it comes to materials,
the buildings' structures,

38
00:01:50,273 --> 00:01:52,025
what holds the buildings up,

39
00:01:52,025 --> 00:01:54,402
really are composed
of four major materials

40
00:01:54,402 --> 00:01:56,321
in every city on the planet:

41
00:01:56,321 --> 00:01:59,240
concrete, steel, masonry and wood.

42
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:01,117
And of those four materials,

43
00:02:01,117 --> 00:02:04,120
three of them have
very, very high carbon footprints,

44
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:06,081
in particular concrete and steel.

45
00:02:06,122 --> 00:02:08,750
Now, wood is the only
material on that list

46
00:02:08,792 --> 00:02:12,128
that's also a renewable resource
and sequesters carbon.

47
00:02:12,170 --> 00:02:14,506
So it's the only pathway as a material

48
00:02:14,547 --> 00:02:17,384
that actually can get us
to carbon-neutral buildings.

49
00:02:17,425 --> 00:02:21,888
And it's the pathway we choose
in our practice to build all buildings.

50
00:02:21,930 --> 00:02:25,767
So our practice has been
a timber-only practice since our inception

51
00:02:25,809 --> 00:02:28,812
and as part of our advocacy
for the use of wood in buildings,

52
00:02:28,853 --> 00:02:32,691
about 15 years ago, I wrote a book
called "The Case for Tall Wood Buildings,"

53
00:02:32,691 --> 00:02:35,652
and that book taught the lesson
of why we should do this

54
00:02:35,652 --> 00:02:36,903
and how we should do this.

55
00:02:36,903 --> 00:02:39,364
And in the beginning,
it was a very unlikely concept

56
00:02:39,364 --> 00:02:41,366
that people had a hard time believing.

57
00:02:41,366 --> 00:02:42,701
And yes, we figured out

58
00:02:42,701 --> 00:02:45,036
that we could have built
the Empire State Building

59
00:02:45,036 --> 00:02:46,329
entirely out of wood.

60
00:02:46,538 --> 00:02:49,416
That early idea
now is a mainstream concept

61
00:02:49,416 --> 00:02:52,419
around the world
in sustainable building practices,

62
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:54,629
and there are hundreds
of tall wood buildings

63
00:02:54,671 --> 00:02:56,005
that have either been built

64
00:02:56,047 --> 00:02:58,258
or are currently under design
and construction,

65
00:02:58,258 --> 00:03:00,009
and thousands more to come.

66
00:03:00,427 --> 00:03:04,431
But wood as a sustainable idea
is not sustainable

67
00:03:04,472 --> 00:03:07,767
unless it actually comes
from sustainable forest practices.

68
00:03:07,809 --> 00:03:11,730
And much of the planet has forests
that we actually need to keep standing

69
00:03:11,730 --> 00:03:13,523
as part of our climate solution.

70
00:03:13,523 --> 00:03:16,943
And in fact, lots of the world
have forests that are under deep threat.

71
00:03:16,943 --> 00:03:20,321
If we think about where population
in the world mostly resides

72
00:03:20,321 --> 00:03:22,157
and where it's growing the quickest,

73
00:03:22,157 --> 00:03:24,743
in fact, most of those areas
either don't have forests

74
00:03:24,743 --> 00:03:27,203
or also are aware
these forests are threatened.

75
00:03:27,203 --> 00:03:29,497
And so wood, as good a solution as it is

76
00:03:29,539 --> 00:03:31,833
and a solution that
I still very much believe in,

77
00:03:31,833 --> 00:03:33,251
is not a global solution.

78
00:03:33,918 --> 00:03:37,046
Most of our cities are still built
in those first three materials:

79
00:03:37,088 --> 00:03:38,840
steel, concrete and masonry,

80
00:03:38,882 --> 00:03:40,800
and they have a high carbon footprint.

81
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:42,969
We're working hard
and people are working hard

82
00:03:42,969 --> 00:03:44,596
to make concrete and steel better.

83
00:03:44,596 --> 00:03:47,098
But unfortunately,
tweaking those existing materials

84
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:48,892
is making them only modestly better.

85
00:03:49,142 --> 00:03:50,435
In the case of concrete,

86
00:03:50,477 --> 00:03:53,480
it's estimated they can reduce
the carbon footprint of concrete

87
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:55,732
at best between 10% and 35%.

88
00:03:55,732 --> 00:03:58,568
And that's a long way to go
from where we need to be,

89
00:03:58,568 --> 00:04:00,653
either at carbon neutrality in our cities,

90
00:04:00,653 --> 00:04:04,491
or better yet, in carbon-sequestering,
carbon-negative buildings.

91
00:04:04,491 --> 00:04:06,785
So how do we get there?

92
00:04:06,785 --> 00:04:09,621
After building about 1.5 million
square feet of mass timber

93
00:04:09,621 --> 00:04:11,456
I still believe in that material,

94
00:04:11,498 --> 00:04:14,250
but I keep asking myself this question:

95
00:04:14,292 --> 00:04:16,920
what is the alternative to “the big four”?

96
00:04:16,961 --> 00:04:19,047
And the answer, in my mind
without question,

97
00:04:19,088 --> 00:04:21,925
is studying and understanding
what's happening in nature

98
00:04:21,966 --> 00:04:23,760
and nature's structures,

99
00:04:23,802 --> 00:04:26,554
combining that with modern biotechnology

100
00:04:26,596 --> 00:04:29,724
and combining that
with all of the computer modeling

101
00:04:29,766 --> 00:04:33,520
that allows us to make incredibly
efficient structures going forward.

102
00:04:33,561 --> 00:04:37,065
All those things together
are creating what we call “Five.”

103
00:04:37,065 --> 00:04:40,401
Five is a fifth way,
a new structural material

104
00:04:40,401 --> 00:04:42,946
that will replace, in my mind over time,

105
00:04:42,946 --> 00:04:44,614
“the big four.”

106
00:04:44,614 --> 00:04:48,576
It's an all-organic material
made from forests and crops

107
00:04:48,576 --> 00:04:50,954
that can be grown all over the world.

108
00:04:50,954 --> 00:04:54,833
It's strong and safe,
and it eventually will be cost-effective

109
00:04:54,874 --> 00:04:57,794
and competitive against
any other material available.

110
00:04:57,836 --> 00:05:00,088
Now, this isn't a new idea.

111
00:05:00,129 --> 00:05:03,716
Products made from natural materials,
thankfully, are growing up everywhere.

112
00:05:03,758 --> 00:05:06,594
There's a huge industry
of biomaterials in academia,

113
00:05:06,636 --> 00:05:08,137
as well as in private business.

114
00:05:08,179 --> 00:05:10,807
And we're seeing new products
come online every day.

115
00:05:10,849 --> 00:05:14,394
This community is strong
and hopefully getting stronger

116
00:05:14,435 --> 00:05:17,647
and our contribution to it
is really focused on this idea

117
00:05:17,647 --> 00:05:22,193
of the biggest material use,
which again is the building structure.

118
00:05:22,193 --> 00:05:23,695
So how does it work?

119
00:05:23,695 --> 00:05:26,072
So the idea is based on the same concept

120
00:05:26,072 --> 00:05:29,492
of how a tree works
or any other vascular plant.

121
00:05:29,492 --> 00:05:34,038
As we zoom in, a tree is sort of
made of lignified tissues

122
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:36,791
that go from the bottom
of the tree to the top,

123
00:05:36,833 --> 00:05:38,251
as are plants.

124
00:05:38,293 --> 00:05:42,005
And those lignified tissues
have within them, in cell structure,

125
00:05:42,046 --> 00:05:44,757
cellulose fibers and lignin

126
00:05:44,799 --> 00:05:49,596
that together make the plant strong
in order to grow and thrive.

127
00:05:49,637 --> 00:05:51,180
And those two materials,

128
00:05:51,222 --> 00:05:54,183
the fiber in the plant,
as well as the lignin,

129
00:05:54,225 --> 00:05:56,060
as well as other binders,

130
00:05:56,102 --> 00:05:59,105
make up the ingredients
of what we call Five.

131
00:05:59,105 --> 00:06:01,482
Studying the fibers
and how they work in plants

132
00:06:01,482 --> 00:06:04,319
is another part of how
we're trying to make this structure.

133
00:06:04,319 --> 00:06:07,739
And if we all imagine cutting through
a branch as the image just showed,

134
00:06:07,739 --> 00:06:11,242
we know that nature finds
very efficient ways to create structures,

135
00:06:11,242 --> 00:06:14,287
and they're often round like a branch.

136
00:06:14,287 --> 00:06:17,373
But as humans, we tend to
make boxy structures.

137
00:06:17,415 --> 00:06:22,003
And the reason we do that is concrete,
steel and these materials and wood,

138
00:06:22,045 --> 00:06:24,964
are more efficient
when we form them as a box

139
00:06:25,006 --> 00:06:26,424
or when we cut them as a box,

140
00:06:26,466 --> 00:06:28,843
It's more affordable
and that's why we see it.

141
00:06:28,885 --> 00:06:33,932
So a typical structure has columns
and beams and a slab that make it up,

142
00:06:33,973 --> 00:06:37,101
and they're very boxy forms,
as you can see in the image.

143
00:06:37,268 --> 00:06:40,438
Now different structures
behave very differently all the time.

144
00:06:40,438 --> 00:06:43,316
This particular structure
is an example from a tall building,

145
00:06:43,316 --> 00:06:46,736
but if we were talking about a house,
it would be a different structure.

146
00:06:46,736 --> 00:06:50,073
Or if we're talking about a hockey rink,
it would be another structure.

147
00:06:50,073 --> 00:06:52,325
So just in this example,
how do the forces work?

148
00:06:52,325 --> 00:06:54,577
Well, you have the force
of the weight of people

149
00:06:54,577 --> 00:06:57,372
and you have the force
of the weight of the building above.

150
00:06:57,372 --> 00:07:00,500
You have wind blowing on the sides
or an earthquake impacting it.

151
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:03,294
The forces, as they press down
on this particular example,

152
00:07:03,294 --> 00:07:07,882
in blue are forces of compression.
That’s the squeezing forces, pushing down.

153
00:07:08,633 --> 00:07:11,844
And the forces here in yellow
are the tension forces,

154
00:07:11,844 --> 00:07:14,347
the pulling apart on that structure.

155
00:07:14,514 --> 00:07:15,848
When we put those together,

156
00:07:15,890 --> 00:07:19,435
we see how the forces move
to the ground through this simple frame.

157
00:07:19,477 --> 00:07:22,021
And it's sort of a flowing diagram.

158
00:07:22,021 --> 00:07:24,983
In fact, when we use
what's called a structural stress plot,

159
00:07:24,983 --> 00:07:28,319
you can see that the form
is very natural in its character.

160
00:07:28,319 --> 00:07:31,072
But as I said,
we build things out of boxes,

161
00:07:31,072 --> 00:07:33,533
and when we overlap in our example,

162
00:07:33,533 --> 00:07:35,743
a boxy structure of wood, for example,

163
00:07:35,743 --> 00:07:38,997
we see these areas in red
which really are unnecessary.

164
00:07:38,997 --> 00:07:41,749
There wasted material
in the current way we build.

165
00:07:41,791 --> 00:07:43,876
In this example,
working with our engineers,

166
00:07:43,918 --> 00:07:46,379
we actually calculated
that this particular example

167
00:07:46,421 --> 00:07:50,341
would result in about 27% material waste.

168
00:07:50,383 --> 00:07:51,926
Now all buildings are different.

169
00:07:51,968 --> 00:07:55,138
It's very hard to make
a sort of overall calculation,

170
00:07:55,179 --> 00:07:57,765
some buildings are more efficient,
some less efficient.

171
00:07:57,807 --> 00:08:00,143
But if we multiply this
by the amount of building

172
00:08:00,184 --> 00:08:03,896
that needs to be built over
the next 40 years for humanity,

173
00:08:03,896 --> 00:08:06,399
it's an incredible amount of human waste.

174
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:09,318
And poor use of our resources.

175
00:08:10,319 --> 00:08:14,032
So our goal with Five is how do we use
as little resources as possible?

176
00:08:14,032 --> 00:08:16,325
And when you use
little resources in a building,

177
00:08:16,325 --> 00:08:18,244
you lighten the building up,

178
00:08:18,244 --> 00:08:21,748
and a lighter building actually means
that it weighs less on the ground,

179
00:08:21,789 --> 00:08:25,001
and our foundations below the ground
can be lighter as well.

180
00:08:25,626 --> 00:08:30,298
So less resources equals lighter buildings
equals even less resources.

181
00:08:30,339 --> 00:08:32,759
Taking this idea of how Five can use less,

182
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:37,263
we took four existing known solutions
and put them together.

183
00:08:37,305 --> 00:08:39,849
The first is we take
those plant fibers I described

184
00:08:39,891 --> 00:08:44,103
and that can come from trees,
that can come from plants, grass, bamboo.

185
00:08:44,145 --> 00:08:45,938
It can come from waste wood products.

186
00:08:45,938 --> 00:08:49,108
It can come from clearing
the understory of a forest

187
00:08:49,108 --> 00:08:53,237
to make it less likely for forest fire
in places like California.

188
00:08:53,237 --> 00:08:58,159
We take those fibers and combine them
with other organic binders and lignin

189
00:08:58,159 --> 00:09:01,329
in order to sort of
create a solid product.

190
00:09:01,329 --> 00:09:03,247
And then we take these computer models

191
00:09:03,289 --> 00:09:06,751
that allow us to really design efficiency
into the way we build

192
00:09:06,793 --> 00:09:09,170
so we can boil out all that waste.

193
00:09:09,212 --> 00:09:11,714
And then finally,
we're going to use custom robotics.

194
00:09:11,756 --> 00:09:13,758
That means that every part of a building,

195
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:16,594
rather than being cookie cutter
and the same and wasteful,

196
00:09:16,636 --> 00:09:19,222
could be just as much material
as needed and no more

197
00:09:19,263 --> 00:09:21,307
in order to make the building safe.

198
00:09:21,349 --> 00:09:23,810
Together, that makes something
that looks like this.

199
00:09:23,851 --> 00:09:25,353
And again, this is an example.

200
00:09:25,394 --> 00:09:28,439
The flat top is flat
because we walk on floors,

201
00:09:28,439 --> 00:09:31,526
but underneath the building
looks more organic in its shape,

202
00:09:31,526 --> 00:09:33,611
and as you can see,
these beams and columns

203
00:09:33,611 --> 00:09:37,949
feel a little bit more like branches
and more like what we see in nature.

204
00:09:37,949 --> 00:09:40,034
As we zoom in even further,

205
00:09:40,034 --> 00:09:42,870
we start to see
how it's actually composed.

206
00:09:42,870 --> 00:09:45,289
And you may think in your mind:
is this fiberboard?

207
00:09:45,331 --> 00:09:47,750
Is this particleboard, things
you’ve seen before?

208
00:09:47,792 --> 00:09:49,710
Well, actually what we're talking about

209
00:09:49,752 --> 00:09:52,380
is a very microscopic layering
of these plant fibers

210
00:09:52,421 --> 00:09:54,006
that are just a millimeter long.

211
00:09:54,048 --> 00:09:55,466
By laying them together

212
00:09:55,508 --> 00:09:58,261
and cross-laminating them
across each other,

213
00:09:58,302 --> 00:10:02,598
we can customize the way,
based again on a structural model,

214
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:06,227
exactly how the composition
of each piece of the material works

215
00:10:06,269 --> 00:10:08,479
in order to make
it as efficient as possible.

216
00:10:08,479 --> 00:10:12,233
We combine it with these
organic polymers and lignin

217
00:10:12,233 --> 00:10:15,653
and ultimately make
this material as strong, dense,

218
00:10:15,653 --> 00:10:17,905
and it behaves much like a tree would.

219
00:10:17,905 --> 00:10:21,534
Now we do that in another unique way
in the way the forming works.

220
00:10:21,534 --> 00:10:24,245
Because no pieces of a structure
have to be the same

221
00:10:24,245 --> 00:10:26,080
and we want to reduce waste,

222
00:10:26,122 --> 00:10:28,666
we use robotic forms and fabric forms.

223
00:10:28,708 --> 00:10:30,626
The red is fabric that moves in and out

224
00:10:30,668 --> 00:10:34,922
and allows each piece of the building
to be completely, uniquely customizable.

225
00:10:34,964 --> 00:10:38,009
And again, we're using it
to reduce the waste of the structure.

226
00:10:38,926 --> 00:10:40,595
Now when we put that all together,

227
00:10:40,636 --> 00:10:43,055
it looks like something
completely different,

228
00:10:43,097 --> 00:10:45,141
something that none of us
have seen before.

229
00:10:45,183 --> 00:10:47,226
It's not like steel,
it's not like concrete

230
00:10:47,268 --> 00:10:48,519
and it's not like wood.

231
00:10:48,561 --> 00:10:53,649
Instead, in this example, what you see
is an entirely plant-based structure.

232
00:10:53,649 --> 00:10:56,611
It's healthy and beautiful to be around.

233
00:10:56,611 --> 00:10:58,654
Its shapes are not there as decoration.

234
00:10:58,654 --> 00:11:00,823
They're there
as just structural essentials,

235
00:11:00,823 --> 00:11:02,491
and yet they're beautiful.

236
00:11:02,491 --> 00:11:04,452
It's safe to be within.

237
00:11:04,452 --> 00:11:05,786
It sequesters carbon,

238
00:11:05,786 --> 00:11:08,289
so it's part of our solution
for climate change.

239
00:11:08,831 --> 00:11:13,211
I believe we cannot continue
to work with broken systems

240
00:11:13,252 --> 00:11:14,754
and try to make them better.

241
00:11:14,795 --> 00:11:16,964
We have to imagine something next.

242
00:11:17,006 --> 00:11:19,675
We also can't wait
for the world's resources to run out

243
00:11:19,717 --> 00:11:21,844
before we imagine a future
that's different.

244
00:11:23,054 --> 00:11:26,891
We are no longer part
of an industrial revolution of materials.

245
00:11:26,933 --> 00:11:30,394
We are at the beginning of Mother Nature's
revolution of materials.

246
00:11:31,312 --> 00:11:36,567
And as a result, we can make much more
beautiful environments for everyone.

247
00:11:36,567 --> 00:11:39,362
As I walk around the forest of my home

248
00:11:39,362 --> 00:11:41,322
and I look up into the trees

249
00:11:41,322 --> 00:11:44,325
and imagine what the buildings
of the future are,

250
00:11:44,325 --> 00:11:47,745
I imagine that they'll use less resources,
they'll have less impact.

251
00:11:47,745 --> 00:11:51,040
They will still make strong,
healthy communities for all of us.

252
00:11:52,124 --> 00:11:53,834
But they'll do so more efficiently.

253
00:11:55,169 --> 00:11:58,339
All of these ideas
are ideas that already exist.

254
00:11:58,381 --> 00:11:59,715
There's nothing new here.

255
00:11:59,757 --> 00:12:03,094
We have an opportunity to live
in a completely biological world.

256
00:12:03,135 --> 00:12:05,721
We just have to decide to do so.

257
00:12:05,763 --> 00:12:08,224
There is a community growing of companies

258
00:12:08,266 --> 00:12:11,310
and products of biomaterials
that are available,

259
00:12:11,352 --> 00:12:15,106
and every day there are new materials
introduced into the system.

260
00:12:15,106 --> 00:12:20,486
We can and we will solve this combination
of human need and the planet's need

261
00:12:20,486 --> 00:12:22,154
at the same time.

262
00:12:22,154 --> 00:12:26,367
All we need to do is listen to nature
and let her teach us how.

263
00:12:26,367 --> 00:12:27,660
Thank you.

264
00:12:27,660 --> 00:12:29,412
(Applause)

